# Appends the evening block of 2026-10-24 to DEVELOPER_NOTES.md (section 2.64): the owner's later asks and what was built. Run once, after the code is applied.
param([switch]$Dry)
$ErrorActionPreference = 'Stop'
$path = 'C:\Users\phick\OneDrive\Desktop\BuildingEscape\DEVELOPER_NOTES.md'
$t = [IO.File]::ReadAllText($path)
if ($t.Contains('**2026-10-24, evening:')) { throw 'the evening block is in the notes already' }
$block = @'

**2026-10-24, evening: the owner's later asks (build `Playtest_2026-10-24e`; patches in `third_agent\`: `patch_scan_popup.ps1`, `patch_audio_options.ps1`, `patch_inventory_hint.ps1`, `patch_outpost_checklist.ps1`, `patch_escape_menu_fit.ps1`, `patch_guard_alert.ps1`, and the test fixes `patch_guard_alert_tests.ps1` / `patch_etr_doors.ps1`).** The owner's rule for the guard list: "Any changes mentioned here should be applied to all floors but specifics will be provided by us ... if we don't provide just use a placeholder for now".
- **Scan pop-ups** ("Scanning should only have a pop-up when it says 'Item Marked', if an item is not found while scanning there should not be a pop-up, when trying to scan on cooldown there should not be a pop-up but instead use one of our sound effect assets to make an error noise"): `LootScanner.OnGUI` draws ONE pill, "Item Marked" (`LootScanRules.MarkedText`), for 2 s after a pulse marked something (the last 0.4 s fade out: `MarkedPopupAlpha`; `LootScanner.PopupText` is what is on screen); the status line (SCANNING / nothing to steal / NO ROOM HERE / THE ELEVATOR), the amber "Wait n seconds" pill and "+n more out of view" are gone (`StatusLine` stays as a read-out for the tests only; `WaitSeconds` / `WaitText` of 2.64 are removed). A scan that marks nothing shows nothing. A PRESS of Z while the 2 s cooldown holds the scan back plays `SoundId.SafeWrong` (our generated "wrong code" blips, flat, volume 0.7), once per press, not while the key stays down (`LootScanRules.RefusedPress`, `LootScanner.ErrorBeeps`). CHECKED: pure + `Asks1023` (27 PASS: no error sound for the scan itself or for a held key, one for a press in the cooldown, no pop-up after a room change). NOT heard by a person. Words: "Item Marked" is the owner's.
- **Audio options** ("Please add Audio Options so players can choose the audio input/output devices they want to use in-game"): `SettingsPanel` (the lobby's and the Esc menu's Settings page) has an AUDIO DEVICES section. MICROPHONE: a list of the devices Windows reports (`Microphone.devices`, looked up every 2 s: `AudioDevices`), DEFAULT first; the pick is saved BY NAME (`PlayerPrefs OvertimeEscape.MicDevice`; a pick that is unplugged counts as DEFAULT until it is back); `VoiceChat.PickInput` opens a picked microphone itself (`MicrophoneVoiceInput(device)`: plain 16 kHz PCM, about 32 KB/s while talking) because Steam's voice recording takes only the device Windows uses by default; with DEFAULT it is Steam's voice as before. A pick that will not open falls back to the first device. Changing the pick while the microphone is open switches it at once. SPEAKERS: Unity 6000.0 has NO way to choose the output device (it plays through the Windows default and follows it), so the page has a button, WINDOWS SOUND SETTINGS, that opens `ms-settings:apps-volume` (App volume and device preferences: the output device of THIS game is picked there; the page ID exists on this Windows 11, the button was not pressed). CHECKED: pure (`AudioDevicesTest`) + pictures of the page (`AudioDevicesUi`: three microphones, no microphone). NOT checked: a real microphone switch (the QA never opens the owner's microphone), the Windows page. Words (placeholders, the owner writes them): AUDIO DEVICES, MICROPHONE, SPEAKERS, DEFAULT, NO MICROPHONE FOUND, WINDOWS SOUND SETTINGS.
- **"Press Tab for inventory"** above "Press J for journal" at the bottom left (`IntelScreens.DrawJournalHint`; same conditions: on a floor, no screen up). Picture checked (`be_ui_floor.png`).
- **The Outpost on the checklist** ("Add the Outpost to the journal checklist"): a fifth line, `ScanKind.Outpost` / `ChecklistRules.OutpostLabel` = "Outpost hacked" (PLACEHOLDER words). THE OWNER DID NOT SAY WHAT COMPLETES IT: it is done once the outpost's lock has been hacked open (`OutpostController.Unlocked`, the state every machine shares) and stays done like the other lines; a floor without an outpost has it done at once. Change `FloorChecklist.Remaining` for another meaning (e.g. "entered" / "its safe opened"). CHECKED: pure + `Checklist` (14 PASS, the journal picture with five ticked lines).
- **The Esc menu fits the screen** ("scale down the size of the Escape menu UI so that it doesn't overlay with the other UIs"): the Settings card went over the floor's banner at the top and the hotbar at the bottom. `MenuLayoutRules`: the cards stand in the band between 130 reference px from the top (the banner and the ribbon that sticks out) and 132 from the bottom (hotbar + stamina bar); the Settings card is 780 tall at most (it was 880) and scrolls. CHECKED: pure (every window height 540 to 2160) + `AudioDevicesUi` pictures (`be_ui_audio_*.png`, `be_ui_esc_main.png`). The Keybinds page still takes the whole window.
- **THE GUARD LIST** (`AlertRules` / `AlertState` pure, `HeistManager`, `GuardAI`, `RunSync.State`, `OutpostController`; scenario `GuardAlert` 24 checks, pure `AlertRulesTest`):
  - MARKS: a guard whose radio call (a sighting, or a bullet / blow he got) is through marks THE PLAYER HE SPOTTED and nobody else (`HeistManager.CallBackup(by, spotted)` -> `MarkSpotted`); an alarm (the lock) or a body marks nobody. A guard who is chasing keeps a notice meter of the players who are not marked yet (`ScanForPlayers(true)`): the second player he notices is marked too, and a chasing guard only changes to a MARKED player (`FindVisibleFreePlayer`; before, he changed to any free player in view, which is what marked the whole team). The backup officers of the repeating call go for a marked free player (the team, as before, when nobody is marked: an alarm).
  - BIG ALARM: half of the party marked (rounded up: 1 of 1, 2 of 2 (a duo needs both), 2 of 3, 2 of 4, 3 of 5) -> `BigAlarmActive` (host, synced to the clients as `vBigAlarm`), banner "BIG ALARM" (PLACEHOLDER words), never off until the floor ends: `StandDown()` and the alarm panel refuse ("Alarm panel - the Big Alarm cannot be shut off" and a notice: PLACEHOLDER words). A SOLO PLAYER'S FIRST SIGHTING IS THE BIG ALARM (he is 100 % of the party): the owner's rule taken literally, to be confirmed.
  - CAPS: `AlertRules.GuardCap`: floors 1 and 2: 6 alive at once, 8 with the Big Alarm; floors 3 to 13: 13 (both: the owner gave one number). It limits the backup officers (the first call, the repeating call) and the replacements; `GuardRules.MaxGuardsAlive` (24) is only the ceiling above it now. THE STARTING PATROLS ARE NOT CUT: floors 9 to 13 start with 14 to 19 guards, above 13, so a backup call sends nobody there ("the floor is already full").
  - REPLACEMENTS: a guard who goes down is replaced by one out of a stairwell after 45 s while the backup is not on, 20 s while it is (`AlertRules.RespawnSeconds`; the wait is judged by the alert AS IT IS when the time is up, so the alert coming on shortens it and going off lengthens it again), one new guard per eliminated guard, none when the cap is reached. Floors 3 and up: the same 45 / 20 (PLACEHOLDER: the owner gave floors 1 and 2 only). The old 15 s (`GuardRules.ReplacementSeconds`) is gone.
  - THE ALERT GOES OFF when somebody is marked, none of the marked players is free (pinned or busted) and the Big Alarm is not on (`HeistManager.TickAlert`): the backup clock stops (no more officers), the marks are cleared, banner "ALL QUIET ..." (the old stand-down words), no radio jam (that is the panel's). Then the marks start again from nothing (2 of 5 marked is no Big Alarm, the third is).
  - CHECKED (scratch copy, offline, one player; the party size is faked with `HeistManager.TestPartySize` where more are needed): see the numbers in the hand-off below. NOT CHECKED: a real second player (a guard noticing a second player while chasing, two machines), the Big Alarm banner / panel text on a client, a long run with the repeating officers up to the cap. Two old tests were told about the caps (Outpost "two officers" -> as many as the cap leaves room for; Pew "backup guards are armed too" -> the Big Alarm first); `NetDevEtr` was told about the pack's glass doors (its 4 FAILs were stale since 2026-10-23). `GuardSight` fails 10 checks (the notice meter of its test guard reads 0): the SAME 10 on the build-d code in `C:\bqfinal`, so it is old and unrelated; nobody has looked at why.
  - QUESTIONS FOR THE OWNER: (1) a solo player's first sighting = Big Alarm: intended? (2) what completes the Outpost line? (3) the respawn times of floors 3 to 13; the Big Alarm cap of floors 3 to 13 (13 as well?); (4) should the starting patrols of floors 9 to 13 be cut to 13? (5) one replacement per eliminated guard (what I built) or keep spawning until the cap is full? (6) is there to be a visible mark on a marked player (the HUD / nametag)? None is drawn.
'@
$block = $block.Replace("`r`n", "`n")
if ($Dry) { "dry ok: would append $($block.Length) characters to $path"; return }
[IO.File]::WriteAllText($path, $t + $block, (New-Object Text.UTF8Encoding($false)))
"appended the evening block to $path"
